# Why per-core caches exist

# Instead of one global cache shared by all cores (which would require CAS/locks for every access), DPDK creates a per-core cache.

# This cache is static in size: RTE_MEMPOOL_CACHE_MAX_SIZE.

# Each lcore gets its own cache during mempool creation.

# --------------------------------------------------

# 2️⃣ What the per-core cache stores

# The cache stores pointers to free objects only.

# Already allocated objects that the application is using are never touched.

# The global ring also stores free object pointers.

# --------------------------------------------------

# 3️⃣ How allocation works

# Core requests an object:

# If the per-core cache is not empty, it pops a pointer from the cache → fast, lockless.

# If the cache is empty, the core fetches multiple free pointers in bulk from the global ring → refill cache → pop one object for immediate use.

# Only free objects are ever moved between cache and global ring.

# Objects already in use are safe.

# --------------------------------------------------

# 4️⃣ Freeing objects

# Freed objects are pushed back into the per-core cache → fast, lockless.

# If the cache is full, some objects are returned in bulk to the global ring.

# --------------------------------------------------

# 5️⃣ External caches

# Internal caches are per-lcore → only usable by EAL-managed threads.

# Non-EAL threads (e.g., CLI, control-plane, debugging, tracing, policy updates) cannot use internal caches.

# For them, DPDK provides external caches:

# Created via rte_mempool_cache_create()

# Freed via rte_mempool_cache_free()

# These caches store free object pointers for non-lcore threads, allowing them to allocate and free objects efficiently without contending with the global ring.

